-- =============================================================================================
-- BizApps Sales v5.2.x -- the metadata seed
-- =============================================================================================
-- Generated by `mj sync push --dir metadata --ci` against MJ_Sales_Pub: a database built from
-- migrations ONLY -- MJ core, bizapps-common, bizapps-tasks, bizapps-accounting, bizapps-orders,
-- then this app's own migrations -- with no prior sync of this app's metadata. The push reported
-- 0 errors: 97 created, 50 updated, 0 skipped.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE HAS TO EXIST. `mj-app.json`'s `metadata.directory` is documentation and nothing
-- else -- MJ's manifest schema says so, and the install engine never reads it. Seeding happens
-- exclusively through `migrations/`. Until this file, every one of the 22 directories under
-- `metadata/` shipped NOWHERE: a clean `mj app install` produced a database with all 20 tables,
-- every view and every CRUD proc, and no deal status types, no forecast categories, no pipelines,
-- no stages, no queries, no actions, no remote operations and no application -- which is to say
-- nothing anybody could open. Every install step reported success.
--
-- `scripts/check-distribution-seed.mjs` is the gate that keeps this file current; regenerate it
-- and `migrations/metadata-seed.manifest.json` together whenever `metadata/` changes.
--
-- ── THE ONE SUBSTITUTION APPLIED TO THE GENERATOR'S OUTPUT ──────────────────────────────────
--
-- (There is also exactly ONE hand-edited statement, marked in place near the `spCreateCompany`
--  call: it is the only create in this file that teardown does not reverse, so it is the only one
--  that has to survive a reinstall. The reasoning is at the edit and in
--  `migrations-teardown/V001__Retire_Sales_Core_Rows.sql`; nothing else in this file is touched.)
--
--   `[__mj]` -> `[${mjSchema}]`, in all 419 places it appeared.
--
--   Both kinds of occurrence mean the same thing and both must follow a host that has renamed its
--   core schema: the core stored-procedure calls (`spCreateQuery`, `spUpdateEntityRelationship`,
--   ...) and the `LEFT OUTER JOIN [__mj].Employee` lines inside the SQL text of the seeded
--   queries. The second kind is data rather than a statement, and it is substituted anyway -- a
--   query whose stored text names a schema the host does not have is a report that returns an
--   error the first time somebody runs it, and nothing earlier would have said so.
--
--   `${flyway:defaultSchema}` is NOT used. This app's own objects are named literally
--   (`[__mj_BizAppsSales]`) everywhere in `migrations/`, because the schema comes from
--   `mj-app.json` and a host cannot rename it; the seed follows the same convention as the
--   baseline rather than introducing a second one. Two sibling schemas are named literally for
--   the same reason and are NOT ours to parameterise: `[__mj_BizAppsTasks].spCreateTaskType`
--   (2 calls -- the Sales Follow-Up and Sales Approval task types, which is why bizapps-tasks is
--   a declared dependency at >=1.2.0: `TaskType.Code` arrives in 1.2.x) and the
--   `__mj_BizAppsOrders` / `__mj_BizAppsCommon` references inside two queries' SQL.
--
--   Bare `__mj.` survives in exactly two places, both prose -- a comment and a
--   `TechnicalDescription` explaining that the slippage report reads MJ's audit trail. Left alone
--   on purpose: substituting inside prose would be substituting inside a sentence.
--
-- ── WHAT MAKES THIS REPLAYABLE ON A DATABASE THAT IS NOT THIS ONE ───────────────────────────
--
-- All 424 `spCreate*` calls pass an explicit `@ID` -- every seeded row carries a hardcoded UUID in
-- its `metadata/<dir>/*.json`, which is the convention this repo already followed for a different
-- reason (pushing twice updates one row rather than creating two).
--
-- The 50 `spUpdate*` calls are the part that could have gone wrong and did not. They do NOT seed
-- rows; they fill columns on rows the baseline's CodeGen half already created -- 18 `Entity` rows
-- (form chrome) and 32 `EntityRelationship` rows (the typed writable child collections on Deal).
-- An update keyed by an ID that CodeGen had generated with NEWID() on the machine that ran the
-- push would target nothing on anybody else's host, and would do it silently. Checked rather than
-- assumed: every one of those 50 IDs is pinned as a literal in
-- `V202608042101__v0.1.x__Tables_and_Objects.sql`, so the rows this file updates are the rows the
-- baseline created, on every host. (The check is case-insensitive -- CodeGen writes its UUIDs
-- lowercase and MetadataSync reads them back uppercase.)
--
-- ORDERING. This file must stay AFTER the baseline: the `spUpdate*` calls need those Entity and
-- EntityRelationship rows to exist, and the `spCreate*` calls for the ten type tables need their
-- tables. Filename order gives that for free and is the only thing enforcing it.
-- =============================================================================================

